{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"3972e979-5e75-4953-aa06-0c7c970352c1","agentId":"a5c3145ddf1f0dffe","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"You are building **Phase 4** of the Baylor play-call study: **alignment and formation vs play-call outcome**.\n\n## Your workspace — do not leave it\n\nWork ONLY in this git worktree:\n`/Users/kristinarash/DEV/PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA/.worktrees/phase4-alignment`\n\nBranch: `phase4-alignment` (already created off `main`, already checked out).\nPython: `.venv/bin/python` in that directory (already built with uv, 76 tests already pass).\nNever `cd` to `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA` — another agent is working there on Phase 2 in parallel.\n\nDo NOT merge, rebase, push, or delete any branch. Commit to `phase4-alignment` only. Leave it for the user to merge.\n\n## Read these first, in this order\n\n1. `CLAUDE.md` in the worktree — the pipeline, the gates, and six traps that each fail SILENTLY rather than erroring. Learn all six.\n2. `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-09-playcalls-success-rate-epa-design.md` — the Phase 1 design. §6.0 is the most useful single section.\n3. `outputs/REPORT.md` — the finished Phase 1 report.\n4. `src/playcalls/analysis.py` and `src/playcalls/charting.py` — read them to reuse their helpers. Do not edit them (see the boundary rule below).\n\nDo not re-derive what those already record.\n\n## The Phase 1 finding you are extending\n\nBaylor offense, 2024 vs 2025. Same offensive coordinator, same starting QB.\nOverall success rate fell 57.4% → 49.5%. The sharpest cut: **early-down running fell 61.7% → 42.7%**. Explosive rate halved, 14.8% → 7.4%. Success with a clean pocket also fell (59.9% → 53.8%), so pressure does not explain it. Phase 1 concluded the cause is not decidable from its data and said so rather than guessing.\n\n**Your job is to test whether alignment explains it.** Did Baylor line up differently in 2025? Did defenses line up differently against them? Did the same play call from the same look stop working, or did the mix of looks change?\n\nThat framing matters. There are two competing explanations and your tables must separate them:\n- **Mix shift** — Baylor used worse-performing formations more often in 2025.\n- **Efficacy drop** — the same formation running the same concept simply produced less.\n\nA decomposition that splits the overall decline into a mix component and an efficacy component is the single most valuable table you can produce. Do that.\n\n## Data available to you\n\n**Source A — the coaches' charting CSV** (`2024_All_Games.csv`, `2025_All_Games.csv` in the worktree root). Already loaded by `src/playcalls/charting.py`. Alignment-relevant columns include:\n`Personnel`, `Formation`, `Form Fam`, `Form Adjust`, `Motion`, `Gen Mo`, `Shifts`, `Tempo`, `RB Align`, `pff_TEALIGNMENT`, `QB#`, `Front`, `Front Adjust`, `Stunt`, `Pressure`, `Coverage`, `Cover Family`, `pff_DEFPERSONNEL`, `pff_BOXPLAYERS`, `Hash_O`.\nNOTE: `src/playcalls/charting.py` currently only surfaces a subset of these. You will need to widen its column selection — that is the ONE exception to the no-edit rule below, and keep the edit strictly additive (add columns, change nothing existing).\n\n**Source B — PFF `team_play`**, read-only from `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Football/PFF_Data_Project/data/pff.db` (DuckDB, not SQLite). Use the existing `src/playcalls/pff.py` loader. Alignment fields inside `raw_json`:\n`offense_package`, `defense_package`, `shotgun`, `pistol`, `motion`, `shift`, `no_huddle`, `play_action`, `screen`, `draw`, `option`, `poa_actual`, `run_concept_1/2/3`, `route`, `pass_zone`, `mofo_coverage_played_shown`, `ball_snapped_from`, `offense_players` (a `;`-delimited jersey list), `defense_players`.\n\n**Source C — the Phase 1 joined table**, `outputs/joined_plays_enriched.parquet` (1,935 rows, tracked in git so it is already in your worktree). This is chart+PFF already joined and validated. **Start here.** It already carries `formation`, `form_fam`, `motion`, `personnel`, `front`, `cover_family`, `box_count`, `def_package`, `shotgun`, `play_action`, `screen`, `draw`, `no_huddle`, `rushers`, `blitz`, `pressured`, `early_down`, `down_distance`, `field_zone`.\n\nDo not re-do the Phase 1 join. Build on its output.\n\n## An open question you must answer, not guess\n\nPFF's `offense_package` looks like `'3-0-1-1'` and `defense_package` like `'3-3-5'`. The defensive one is conventional (DL-LB-DB). **The offensive one is NOT documented in this repo.** Do not assume what its four numbers mean.\n\nResolve it empirically before using it: cross-tabulate `offense_package` against the chart's `Personnel` column (which uses standard RB-TE notation like `11`, `12`, `21`) and against the count of jerseys in `offense_players`. Check `/Users/kristinarash/DEV/Football/PFF_Data_Project/docs` for a data dictionary first. Write what you determined, and the evidence for it, into your spec. If you cannot resolve it confidently, say so and prefer the chart's `Personnel` column instead. **Do not ship a field whose meaning you guessed.**\n\n## Hard boundary — this is how you avoid a merge conflict\n\nPhase 2 is running in parallel and IS editing `analysis.py`, `build_tables.py`, `report.py` and `run.py`. If you touch those, the merge breaks.\n\n**Create new files:**\n- `src/playcalls/alignment.py` — all your logic\n- `build_alignment_tables.py` — top-level script, writes to `outputs/tables/alignment_*.csv`\n- `build_alignment_report.py` — writes `outputs/ALIGNMENT.md`\n- `tests/test_alignment.py`\n\n**Do NOT edit:** `analysis.py`, `build_tables.py`, `report.py`, `run.py`, `join.py`, `pff.py`, `unmatched.py`, `normalise.py`, `gamemap.py`, `build_report.py`, `build_artifact.py`, or any existing test file.\n\n**One exception:** `charting.py`'s column selection may be widened additively, as described above. Nothing else in it changes.\n\n**Do not edit** `CLAUDE.md` or `AGENTS.md`. Instead write your notes into your own spec and say in your final report which lines the user should later add to those two files. (They are kept in sync and must NEVER be symlinked.)\n\n## Outcome metric\n\nUse **success rate** as the outcome now (`charting.success_rate` and `analysis.rate_by` already exist — reuse them, do not reimplement).\n\n**Design every aggregation so EPA drops in later without a rewrite.** Concretely: take the outcome column name and aggregation function as parameters with success-rate defaults, rather than hardcoding success rate inside each function. Phase 2 is adding an `epa` column to the joined table; when it merges, your tables should light up by passing a different argument.\n\n## The six silent traps (from CLAUDE.md — confirm against it)\n\n1. `play_id` is float64 in the joined table. A naive `.astype(str)` gives `\"5642403.0\"` against PFF's `\"5642403\"`, and every merge matches zero rows. Use `analysis._play_key`.\n2. PFF feeds name Baylor inconsistently: `quarterback_charting` uses `team`; `rushing`/`passing`/`team_play` use `offense`. Baylor's code is `TXBA`. Filter both or opponent rows collide on `play_id`.\n3. Coverage data is **dropbacks only**. Run plays take `def_package` from the `rushing` feed. Never pool them.\n4. Pressure is not a column. It is derived from the `pressure` feed's `defensive_grade_type` ∈ (SK, H, P).\n5. `week1_auburn/` in `PFF_Data_Project` is the **2026** season, not 2025. Baylor plays Auburn in both. Always filter on season explicitly.\n6. The 2021–2023 CSVs have **no outcome columns at all**. Success rate is impossible for them. Never pool them into a number.\n\n## Security and repository boundaries\n\n- `PFF_Data_Project` owns all PFF ingest. You open `pff.db` **read-only**. Never call the PFF API. Never read, print, copy or commit `PFF_Data_Project/.env`.\n- The charting data contains proprietary Baylor play-call terminology. Do not publish it anywhere, do not create an Artifact, do not send it to any external service.\n\n## Quality bar — this project has been burned before\n\nPhase 1 shipped with 68 passing tests AND two wrong numbers in a published report, because the tests only tested what had been thought of.\n\nNon-negotiable:\n- **Every number in your prose is computed from a table.** Never type a figure into text. Add regression tests that fail if a hardcoded figure creeps in — three such tests already exist in `tests/test_report.py`; copy their pattern.\n- **Report `n` beside every rate.** Formation cuts fragment fast. Mark any cell with n < 30 as low-n, and do not build a claim on one.\n- **A cut with a small denominator is not a finding.** Say so explicitly in the report where it applies.\n- When alignment does NOT explain the decline, say that plainly. A null result reported honestly is the correct output. Do not hunt for a flattering slice.\n\n## Process — follow it, do not improvise\n\n1. `Skill(\"superpowers:brainstorming\")` — classify the work, then design. This is architectural. Write the spec to `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-09-playcalls-phase4-alignment-design.md` and commit it.\n2. `Skill(\"superpowers:writing-plans\")` — plan to `docs/superpowers/plans/2026-09-09-playcalls-phase4.md`, commit it.\n3. `Skill(\"superpowers:test-driven-development\")` — implement. Tests first.\n4. `Skill(\"superpowers:verification-before-completion\")` — before you declare done.\n\nYou have no human to approve gates. Where brainstorming says \"get user approval\", record your decision and its reasoning in the spec instead and continue. State every assumption you made.\n\nRun the full suite (`.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q`) before every commit. All 76 existing tests must still pass, plus yours.\n\nCommit in logical steps with real reasoning in the message body. End every commit message with exactly:\n\n```\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Cpiq4uQvzv6fct94zkaVCX\n```\n\n## Final report back to me\n\nKeep it short and concrete:\n1. What `offense_package` turned out to mean, and your evidence.\n2. Does alignment explain the 2025 decline? Mix shift, efficacy drop, or neither — with the decomposition numbers.\n3. The three most interesting tables you built, by file path.\n4. What you could not determine and why.\n5. The exact lines the user should add to `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md`.\n6. Anything you found that contradicts the Phase 1 report.\n<context_window_protection>\n  <priority_instructions>\n    Every byte a tool returns enters your conversation memory and costs reasoning capacity for the rest of the session. The context-mode tools let you do the work in a sandbox and surface only the derived answer — the raw bytes stay out. Think-in-Code: program the analysis, do not compute it by reading raw data into your conversation.\n  </priority_instructions>\n\n  <deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n    The context-mode tools below may be DEFERRED in your harness — their schemas are not loaded yet, so calling them directly fails (e.g. \"tool not found\" / InputValidationError). Load them ONCE before your first ctx_* call:\n    ToolSearch(query: \"select:mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file,mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index\")\n    After that they are callable. If any ctx_* call fails as not-found, ToolSearch it and retry — do NOT fall back to Bash/Read just because the schema was not loaded yet.\n  </deferred_tool_bootstrap>\n\n  <tool_selection_hierarchy>\n    0. MEMORY: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(sort: \"timeline\")\n       - On resume or compaction, query prior decisions, errors, plans, user prompts before asking the user — auto-captured session memory is searchable.\n    1. GATHER: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute(commands, queries)\n       - Primary research tool. Runs commands in parallel, auto-indexes each output, and (when queries are passed) returns matching sections in the same round trip — no follow-up search call.\n       - Each command: {label: \"section header\", command: \"shell command\"}; the label becomes the FTS5 chunk title — descriptive labels improve search.\n    2. FOLLOW-UP: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search(queries: [\"q1\", \"q2\", ...])\n       - Multiple related questions about anything already indexed (your captures + session memory). Batch every question in one array; the ranking pipeline runs per-query and the round-trip cost is paid once.\n    3. PROCESSING: mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute(language, code) | mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file(path, language, code)\n       - Derive answers FROM data: filter, count, aggregate, parse, transform. Only what you console.log() enters your conversation; the raw bytes stay in the sandbox.\n  </tool_selection_hierarchy>\n\n  <when_not_to_use>\n    - You intend to PROCESS the output (filter, count, parse, aggregate) → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_batch_execute or mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute. Bash stays correct when you intend to OBSERVE a short fixed output (git status on a clean tree, whoami, pwd) or when you are mutating state (git, mkdir, rm, mv, navigation).\n    - You want to analyze, summarize, or extract from a file → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file. Read stays correct when you intend to Edit the file (Edit needs the exact bytes in your conversation to match against).\n    - WebFetch → use mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_fetch_and_index; full network access, results indexed for mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_search, raw page bytes never enter your conversation.\n    - mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute and mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file for file writes → these run code in a subprocess and discard the sandbox FS; they are for analysis, processing, and computation only.\n  </when_not_to_use>\n\n  <file_writing_policy>\n    File writes use the native Write or Edit tool — mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute, mcp__plugin_context-mode_context-mode__ctx_execute_file, and Bash subprocesses do not persist edits to the host filesystem.\n    Applies to all file types: code, configs, plans, specs, YAML, JSON, markdown.\n  </file_writing_policy>\n\n  <output_constraints>\n    <artifact_policy>\n      Write artifacts (code, configs, PRDs) to files. Return only: file path + 1-line description.\n    </artifact_policy>\n  </output_constraints>\n  <session_continuity>\n    Skills, roles, and decisions captured earlier in this session are a memory aid, not a standing order. Treat them as context that may help — the user's most recent message always takes precedence. If a captured directive conflicts with what the user now asks, follow the user; a past phrase does not bind you.\n  </session_continuity>\n\n</context_window_protection>"},"uuid":"94a24681-efae-4938-901a-2035db26f25b","timestamp":"2026-09-10T03:14:19.421Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA/.worktrees/phase4-alignment","sessionId":"b8143861-beec-4930-9c27-06bc9d94fa8b","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"main"}
