{"parentUuid":"7a15ea57-f8bb-4436-a9d7-24c2131ca091","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a5c3145ddf1f0dffe","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Ceu2jk21dU1Tjz6EGtogP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Eo947qntfdM15n3QMetXp7","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n '239,300p;388,440p' docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-09-playcalls-success-rate-epa-design.md","description":"Read spec sections 6.0 and 6.3-6.4"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1490,"cache_read_input_tokens":66362,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1490,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Ceu2jiqpEbZa4Hr5A7Ndh","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","attributionSkill":"superpowers:using-git-worktrees","attributionPlugin":"superpowers","type":"assistant","uuid":"afabad8c-1032-43bb-8f05-6d10b1220a7d","timestamp":"2026-09-10T03:14:40.388Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA/.worktrees/phase4-alignment","sessionId":"b8143861-beec-4930-9c27-06bc9d94fa8b","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"afabad8c-1032-43bb-8f05-6d10b1220a7d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"3972e979-5e75-4953-aa06-0c7c970352c1","agentId":"a5c3145ddf1f0dffe","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Eo947qntfdM15n3QMetXp7","type":"tool_result","content":"## 6. Open data questions — resolved before analysis\n\n### 6.0 RESOLVED 2026-09-09 — both gates passed\n\n**The rushing gap did not exist.** It was a comparison error in this spec's own\nresearch, corrected here rather than quietly dropped.\n\nThe \"20 charted runs vs 43 PFF runs\" figure in §6.1 compared Baylor's **2025** charting\nfile against the PFF pull sitting in `PFF_Data_Project/data/week1_auburn/`. That folder\nis the **2026** season opener (Baylor at Auburn, 2026-09-05), not the 2025 one. Two\ndifferent games a year apart. Baylor plays Auburn in both seasons, which is what made\nthe mistake easy to miss.\n\nMeasured properly against PFF game 28762 — Baylor vs Auburn, 2025-08-30 — PFF holds\nexactly **20** Baylor rushes for that game. The chart and PFF agree.\n\nAcross all 25 games:\n\n| Season | Charted runs | PFF runs | Charted passes | PFF passes | Shortfall |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2024 | 447 | 448 | 486 | 500 | **1.6%** |\n| 2025 | 382 | 383 | 598 | 597 | **0.0%** |\n\n**Gate results:**\n\n- **Join match rate: 99.3%** across 1,935 charted plays. **17 of 25** games matched\n  100%. Threshold was 95%. The weakest game is 2508 vs Cincinnati at 91.0% (61 of 67).\n- **Charting shortfall: 1.6% worst case** (2024 — 14 pass plays and 1 run, 15 in\n  total). Threshold was 2%. 2025 is complete.\n\nThe 390 unclaimed PFF plays break down as:\n\n| Classification | Count |\n|---|---|\n| `special_teams` | 368 |\n| `unmatched_pass` | 11 |\n| `unknown` | 8 |\n| `unmatched_run` | 3 |\n\nThe 8 `unknown` rows are special teams that PFF left untagged: six are 4th-down punts\nor field-goal attempts with a null `st_play_type`, and two are extra-point attempts\n(`down = 0` at the opponent 3). They appear in neither the rushing nor the passing\nfeed, which is why the classifier could not place them from those. None is a scrimmage\ndown.\n\nOnly 3 rushes and 11 passes are unclaimed scrimmage downs, and since the season totals\nagree those are join misses rather than charting gaps.\n\n**Consequence:** success rates rest on a complete denominator for 2025 and a\n98.4%-complete one for 2024. The 2024 figure carries a small upward bias if the 14\nuncharted passes were disproportionately unsuccessful. The report states this.\n\n**The join hypothesis in §5.2 is confirmed.** Opponent codes match across both sources\nwith zero difference in either season, and 18 of 25 games join perfectly on\ndown/distance/yard-line. The charting tool imported its `pff_`-prefixed columns from\nPFF, so the values agree exactly.\n\n---\n\n### 6.1 The rushing gap — highest priority *(superseded by §6.0)*\n\n2025 game 1:\n### 6.3 Confounds to carry into the report\n\n- **Quarterback.** 2024 used QB 13 (813 snaps) and QB 7 (136 snaps). 2025 is almost\n  entirely QB 13. A 2024-vs-2025 gap partly measures a QB change; splits should show it.\n- **Coordinator — confirmed same, 2024 and 2025** (Eric, 2026-09-09). This is load\n  bearing. It means the 57.4% → 49.5% drop is *not* a change of play-caller, so the\n  2024-vs-2025 comparison is a legitimate read on scheme evolution, personnel and\n  execution under one system. The report should say this explicitly, because a reader\n  will otherwise assume a staff change explains the gap.\n- **Defensive charting collapsed in 2025**, in the Baylor file:\n\n  | Column | 2024 fill | 2025 fill |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | `Front` | 98.3% | **0.0%** |\n  | `Cover Family` | 90.1% | **0.4%** |\n  | `Coverage` | 1.4% | 0.4% |\n  | `Def Play Call` | 0.0% | 2.5% |\n\n  PFF supplies coverage per play, so this is recoverable — but PFF coverage comes from\n  `quarterback_charting`, which covers **dropbacks only**. Run-play defensive context\n  comes from `def_package` in the `rushing` feed instead. The two are not the same\n  field and must not be pooled.\n\n- **Other year-over-year collapses:** `Gen Play` 47.1% → 0.0%; `RB Align` 89.9% → 0.0%;\n  `Group #` 0.0% → 49.2%. None of these support cross-year grouping.\n\n### 6.4 Known cleaning rules\n\n- **UTF-8 BOM** on every header — read with `encoding='utf-8-sig'`.\n- **`Name` contains a comma** inside quotes (`\"2501 BU OFF vs AUB, Play 001\"`). Naive\n  `awk -F,` / `cut -d,` shifts every later column by one. Use a real CSV parser.\n- **`Name` format differs by season** — `YY.GG` in 2021–2022, `YYGG` in 2023–2025, and\n  2021's first game reads `20.01 BU vs TXST OFF` with a different word order.\n- **`pff_FIELDPOSITION` is a signed yard line**, not a distance: `'-34` = own 34,\n  `'+18` = opponent 18. The apostrophe is Excel's text guard. Prefer\n  `pff_YARDSTOGOALLINE`, a clean unsigned integer, verified consistent on all 987 rows\n  of 2025 (`100 − |FP|` own side, `FP` opponent side).\n- **`pff_GAINLOSS`** carries the apostrophe on negatives only.\n- **9 junk rows in 2024** carry drill names in `pff_DEFTEAM` (`HAL SPEED CUT`,\n  `THROW BY JC`, `SPLIT RELEASE`, `m`). Drop them. Effect on 2024 success rate:\n  57.6% → 57.4%.\n- **`Prac Period` is not a practice flag.** Values are `CHECKED` (2024) and alignment\n  tags `OPEN` / `TE WEAK` / `TE STRONG` (2025). Do not filter on it.\n- **`Efficiency = X`** is 1 row in 2025 and 7 in 2024; all are victory-formation kneels\n  or penalties. Exclude `X` and blank from the success-rate denominator.\n- **`EXPL`** is `Y` or blank — blank means not explosive, not missing.\n- **`pff_DRIVEENDEVENT`** is a drive attribute repeated on every play of the drive.\n  `TOUCHDOWN` does not mean *this* play scored.\n- **`pff_DOWN` includes `5`** — 11 rows in 2025, 2 in 2024. Inspect before use.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Analysis deliverables","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"8b5ad3ed-fcb2-46ac-a3db-f59e08cd5eb3","timestamp":"2026-09-10T03:14:40.554Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"## 6. Open data questions — resolved before analysis\n\n### 6.0 RESOLVED 2026-09-09 — both gates passed\n\n**The rushing gap did not exist.** It was a comparison error in this spec's own\nresearch, corrected here rather than quietly dropped.\n\nThe \"20 charted runs vs 43 PFF runs\" figure in §6.1 compared Baylor's **2025** charting\nfile against the PFF pull sitting in `PFF_Data_Project/data/week1_auburn/`. That folder\nis the **2026** season opener (Baylor at Auburn, 2026-09-05), not the 2025 one. Two\ndifferent games a year apart. Baylor plays Auburn in both seasons, which is what made\nthe mistake easy to miss.\n\nMeasured properly against PFF game 28762 — Baylor vs Auburn, 2025-08-30 — PFF holds\nexactly **20** Baylor rushes for that game. The chart and PFF agree.\n\nAcross all 25 games:\n\n| Season | Charted runs | PFF runs | Charted passes | PFF passes | Shortfall |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2024 | 447 | 448 | 486 | 500 | **1.6%** |\n| 2025 | 382 | 383 | 598 | 597 | **0.0%** |\n\n**Gate results:**\n\n- **Join match rate: 99.3%** across 1,935 charted plays. **17 of 25** games matched\n  100%. Threshold was 95%. The weakest game is 2508 vs Cincinnati at 91.0% (61 of 67).\n- **Charting shortfall: 1.6% worst case** (2024 — 14 pass plays and 1 run, 15 in\n  total). Threshold was 2%. 2025 is complete.\n\nThe 390 unclaimed PFF plays break down as:\n\n| Classification | Count |\n|---|---|\n| `special_teams` | 368 |\n| `unmatched_pass` | 11 |\n| `unknown` | 8 |\n| `unmatched_run` | 3 |\n\nThe 8 `unknown` rows are special teams that PFF left untagged: six are 4th-down punts\nor field-goal attempts with a null `st_play_type`, and two are extra-point attempts\n(`down = 0` at the opponent 3). They appear in neither the rushing nor the passing\nfeed, which is why the classifier could not place them from those. None is a scrimmage\ndown.\n\nOnly 3 rushes and 11 passes are unclaimed scrimmage downs, and since the season totals\nagree those are join misses rather than charting gaps.\n\n**Consequence:** success rates rest on a complete denominator for 2025 and a\n98.4%-complete one for 2024. The 2024 figure carries a small upward bias if the 14\nuncharted passes were disproportionately unsuccessful. The report states this.\n\n**The join hypothesis in §5.2 is confirmed.** Opponent codes match across both sources\nwith zero difference in either season, and 18 of 25 games join perfectly on\ndown/distance/yard-line. The charting tool imported its `pff_`-prefixed columns from\nPFF, so the values agree exactly.\n\n---\n\n### 6.1 The rushing gap — highest priority *(superseded by §6.0)*\n\n2025 game 1:\n### 6.3 Confounds to carry into the report\n\n- **Quarterback.** 2024 used QB 13 (813 snaps) and QB 7 (136 snaps). 2025 is almost\n  entirely QB 13. A 2024-vs-2025 gap partly measures a QB change; splits should show it.\n- **Coordinator — confirmed same, 2024 and 2025** (Eric, 2026-09-09). This is load\n  bearing. It means the 57.4% → 49.5% drop is *not* a change of play-caller, so the\n  2024-vs-2025 comparison is a legitimate read on scheme evolution, personnel and\n  execution under one system. The report should say this explicitly, because a reader\n  will otherwise assume a staff change explains the gap.\n- **Defensive charting collapsed in 2025**, in the Baylor file:\n\n  | Column | 2024 fill | 2025 fill |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | `Front` | 98.3% | **0.0%** |\n  | `Cover Family` | 90.1% | **0.4%** |\n  | `Coverage` | 1.4% | 0.4% |\n  | `Def Play Call` | 0.0% | 2.5% |\n\n  PFF supplies coverage per play, so this is recoverable — but PFF coverage comes from\n  `quarterback_charting`, which covers **dropbacks only**. Run-play defensive context\n  comes from `def_package` in the `rushing` feed instead. The two are not the same\n  field and must not be pooled.\n\n- **Other year-over-year collapses:** `Gen Play` 47.1% → 0.0%; `RB Align` 89.9% → 0.0%;\n  `Group #` 0.0% → 49.2%. None of these support cross-year grouping.\n\n### 6.4 Known cleaning rules\n\n- **UTF-8 BOM** on every header — read with `encoding='utf-8-sig'`.\n- **`Name` contains a comma** inside quotes (`\"2501 BU OFF vs AUB, Play 001\"`). Naive\n  `awk -F,` / `cut -d,` shifts every later column by one. Use a real CSV parser.\n- **`Name` format differs by season** — `YY.GG` in 2021–2022, `YYGG` in 2023–2025, and\n  2021's first game reads `20.01 BU vs TXST OFF` with a different word order.\n- **`pff_FIELDPOSITION` is a signed yard line**, not a distance: `'-34` = own 34,\n  `'+18` = opponent 18. The apostrophe is Excel's text guard. Prefer\n  `pff_YARDSTOGOALLINE`, a clean unsigned integer, verified consistent on all 987 rows\n  of 2025 (`100 − |FP|` own side, `FP` opponent side).\n- **`pff_GAINLOSS`** carries the apostrophe on negatives only.\n- **9 junk rows in 2024** carry drill names in `pff_DEFTEAM` (`HAL SPEED CUT`,\n  `THROW BY JC`, `SPLIT RELEASE`, `m`). Drop them. Effect on 2024 success rate:\n  57.6% → 57.4%.\n- **`Prac Period` is not a practice flag.** Values are `CHECKED` (2024) and alignment\n  tags `OPEN` / `TE WEAK` / `TE STRONG` (2025). Do not filter on it.\n- **`Efficiency = X`** is 1 row in 2025 and 7 in 2024; all are victory-formation kneels\n  or penalties. Exclude `X` and blank from the success-rate denominator.\n- **`EXPL`** is `Y` or blank — blank means not explosive, not missing.\n- **`pff_DRIVEENDEVENT`** is a drive attribute repeated on every play of the drive.\n  `TOUCHDOWN` does not mean *this* play scored.\n- **`pff_DOWN` includes `5`** — 11 rows in 2025, 2 in 2024. Inspect before use.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Analysis deliverables","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"afabad8c-1032-43bb-8f05-6d10b1220a7d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA/.worktrees/phase4-alignment","sessionId":"b8143861-beec-4930-9c27-06bc9d94fa8b","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"8b5ad3ed-fcb2-46ac-a3db-f59e08cd5eb3","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a5c3145ddf1f0dffe","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Ceu2jk21dU1Tjz6EGtogP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ENvpuBCQndgtWgNWjs7MwP","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n '1,110p;187,232p;852,870p' outputs/REPORT.md","description":"Read report headline, formation, personnel, limits"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1490,"cache_read_input_tokens":66362,"output_tokens":243,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1490},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":243,"cache_read_input_tokens":66362,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1490,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1490,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Ceu2jiqpEbZa4Hr5A7Ndh","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","attributionSkill":"superpowers:using-git-worktrees","attributionPlugin":"superpowers","type":"assistant","uuid":"b60cf0b5-9cf7-490b-9cf3-eb1f2825aab0","timestamp":"2026-09-10T03:14:41.427Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA/.worktrees/phase4-alignment","sessionId":"b8143861-beec-4930-9c27-06bc9d94fa8b","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"b60cf0b5-9cf7-490b-9cf3-eb1f2825aab0","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"3972e979-5e75-4953-aa06-0c7c970352c1","agentId":"a5c3145ddf1f0dffe","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ENvpuBCQndgtWgNWjs7MwP","type":"tool_result","content":"# Baylor Offensive Play-Call Analysis\n\n## 2024 and 2025, 1,935 charted plays across 25 games\n\n**Audience:** analytics staff. **Scope:** Baylor offensive snaps, 2024 and 2025 only.\n\n---\n\n## The headline\n\nOffensive success rate fell from **57.4%** in 2024 to **49.5%** in 2025 — a drop of **8.0 points**.\n\nExplosive-play rate fell further in relative terms, from **14.8%** to **7.4%** — roughly half.\n\nThe offensive coordinator did not change between these seasons, and Sawyer Robertson took the large majority of snaps at quarterback in both — 806 in 2024, 958 in 2025. The drop is therefore not explained by a change of play-caller or a new starter. It has to be read as scheme, personnel or execution inside one system.\n\n|   season |   plays |   graded | success   | explosive   |\n|---------:|--------:|---------:|:----------|:------------|\n|     2024 |     948 |      928 | 57.4%     | 14.8%       |\n|     2025 |     987 |      986 | 49.5%     | 7.4%        |\n\n---\n\n## Where it broke\n\nFour findings carry most of the decline.\n\n### 1. First down\n\n- **1st & 10** — 60.1% to 43.7%, down **16.4 points**.\n\nThis is the largest single move in the study, and it lands on the most common situation in football — roughly 390 plays in each season, about 40.3% of every snap charted. An offense that cannot win first down spends the rest of the series behind schedule.\n\n### 2. The run game\n\n- **Run** — 63.1% to 50.7%, down **12.4 points**.\n- **Pass** — 52.8% to 49.3%, down **3.5 points**.\n\nThe pass game moved only slightly. The run game fell off a cliff. In 2024 running was clearly the better option; by 2025 the two were level, and the offense had lost the thing it did best.\n\n|   season | run/pass   |   plays |   graded | success   | explosive   |\n|---------:|:-----------|--------:|---------:|:----------|:------------|\n|     2024 | R          |     447 |      444 | 63.1%     | 11.6%       |\n|     2024 | P          |     486 |      479 | 52.8%     | 18.1%       |\n|     2025 | R          |     382 |      381 | 50.7%     | 5.8%        |\n|     2025 | P          |     598 |      598 | 49.3%     | 8.5%        |\n\n### 3. Early-down running\n\n- **Run, 1st and 2nd down** — 61.7% to 42.7%, down **19.0 points**.\n\nThis is the run-game decline at its sharpest. Early-down running is where an offense sets up everything that follows, and it is the single worst cut in the study.\n\n|   season | run/pass   |   plays |   graded | success   | explosive   |\n|---------:|:-----------|--------:|---------:|:----------|:------------|\n|     2024 | R          |     349 |      347 | 61.7%     | 11.8%       |\n|     2024 | P          |     356 |      350 | 54.6%     | 17.4%       |\n|     2025 | P          |     438 |      438 | 50.5%     | 9.6%        |\n|     2025 | R          |     296 |      295 | 42.7%     | 4.0%        |\n\n### 4. The offense stopped running\n\nRun share fell from **47.9%** of scrimmage plays to **39.0%**.\n\nCause and effect are not separable from this data alone. The offense may have abandoned a run game that stopped working, or the run game may have suffered from being used less and in more predictable spots. Both readings fit these numbers. Deciding between them needs film, not this table.\n\n\n---\n\n## How much to trust this\n\n- **Join quality.** 98.6% of charting rows matched a PFF play, with 15 of 25 games matching perfectly. Unmatched rows still count toward success rate; they simply carry no PFF defensive fields.\n- **Charting completeness.** The charting file holds essentially every scrimmage play PFF recorded. Worst-case shortfall is **1.6%** (2024, 15 plays); 2025 is complete. If those uncharted 2024 plays were disproportionately unsuccessful, the 2024 rate is very slightly overstated, which would make the real decline smaller — not larger.\n- **No matched play moves backward in game time**, and no charted play is paired with a kickoff, punt, field goal or extra point. Both are enforced as tests and as gates; an earlier version of this pipeline had three charted plays matched to field goals.\n- **1 charted scrimmage play(s) have no PFF counterpart.** Reported separately from the shortfall, never netted against it — a missing play and a surplus play are two different problems and must not cancel.\n- **Seasons 2021 to 2023 are excluded.** Those files carry no down, distance, yardage or field position. Success rate cannot be computed for them at all. Nothing in this report covers them.\n- **Small groups are suppressed.** Any cut with fewer than 15 graded plays is dropped rather than printed. A high rate on a handful of reps is noise.\n- **`Efficiency` is Baylor's own hand-charted success tag**, not a derived metric. Values `X` (victory-formation kneels, penalties) and blank are excluded from the denominator.\n- **This report contains no EPA.** Expected points needs a model fitted on a large play-by-play corpus, which this project does not have. Nothing here is a proxy for EPA, and no number should be read as one.\n- **Coverage is charted by PFF on dropbacks only.** Run plays carry defensive personnel (`def_package`) instead. The two are never pooled. Baylor's own 2025 defensive charting is effectively absent — `Front` 0% filled, `Cover Family` 0.4% — so every 2025 defensive split here comes from PFF.\n- **Cross-year play-call comparison uses base concepts only.** 2025 charts modifiers 2024 did not, so `RAZOR.BLADE SPLIT` and `RAZOR/BLADE` are compared as one concept.\n\n---\n\n## A labelling change you need to know about\n\nThe `Play Type` vocabulary changed between seasons. This was derived from the data, not assumed: each run concept was tracked across both years and its dominant label compared.\n\n| concept         | play_type_2024   | play_type_2025   |   reps_2024 |   reps_2025 | changed   | confidence   |\n|:----------------|:-----------------|:-----------------|------------:|------------:|:----------|:-------------|\n| RAZOR BLADE     | ZONE             | ZONE             |         115 |         122 | False     | high         |\n| IRENE ITALY     | ZONE             | ZONE             |         133 |         108 | False     | high         |\n| MARK MOLLY      | ZONE             | ZONE             |          39 |         100 | False     | high         |\n| GARY GAIL       | CTR              | GAP              |          29 |          70 | True      | high         |\n| RABBIT LETTUCE  | WIDE ZONE        | OZ               |          30 |          39 | True      | high         |\n| COREY CLEVELAND | CTR              | GAP              |          66 |          36 | True      | high         |\n\n`CTR` became `GAP`. `WIDE ZONE` became `OZ`. `ZONE` stayed `ZONE`. These are the same plays under new labels, so any table below that groups by `Play Type` will show 2024 and 2025 categories that look different but are not.\n\n---\n\n## Where the offense won and lost\n\n\n### Down and distance\n\nNote that 3rd & long actually improved. The problem is earlier in the series.\n\n|   season | down & dist   |   plays |   graded | success   | explosive   |\n|---------:|:--------------|--------:|---------:|:----------|:------------|\n### Formation family\n\n|   season | formation   |   plays |   graded | success   | explosive   |\n|---------:|:------------|--------:|---------:|:----------|:------------|\n|     2024 | TRIO        |      15 |       15 | 73.3%     | 26.7%       |\n|     2024 | UNBALANCED  |      46 |       45 | 62.2%     | 4.3%        |\n|     2024 | EMPTY       |      28 |       26 | 61.5%     | 25.0%       |\n|     2024 | DBL STK     |      18 |       18 | 61.1%     | 11.1%       |\n|     2024 | ERL/LTE     |     239 |      237 | 59.9%     | 14.6%       |\n|     2025 | GRN/GLD     |      17 |       17 | 58.8%     | 17.6%       |\n|     2024 | RIP/LIZ     |     202 |      198 | 58.1%     | 14.8%       |\n|     2024 | TRIPS       |     105 |      105 | 57.1%     | 14.3%       |\n|     2025 | UNBAL       |      39 |       39 | 56.4%     | 2.6%        |\n|     2024 | DUO         |     196 |      192 | 55.2%     | 18.4%       |\n|     2025 | DUO         |     291 |      291 | 53.6%     | 6.5%        |\n|     2025 | TRIPS       |     157 |      157 | 51.6%     | 5.7%        |\n|     2025 | RIP/LIZ     |     179 |      179 | 49.7%     | 10.1%       |\n|     2025 | ERL/LTE     |     166 |      166 | 45.2%     | 9.6%        |\n|     2025 | TRIO        |      37 |       37 | 40.5%     | 5.4%        |\n\n### Personnel\n\n|   season | personnel   |   plays |   graded | success   | explosive   |\n|---------:|:------------|--------:|---------:|:----------|:------------|\n|     2024 | 12          |      67 |       66 | 59.1%     | 3.0%        |\n|     2025 | 12S         |      24 |       24 | 58.3%     | 4.2%        |\n|     2024 | 11          |     845 |      832 | 57.8%     | 15.9%       |\n|     2025 | 12          |      18 |       18 | 50.0%     | 5.6%        |\n|     2025 | 11          |     931 |      931 | 49.1%     | 7.6%        |\n|     2024 | 10          |      30 |       29 | 41.4%     | 13.3%       |\n\n### Field zone\n\n|   season | field_zone   |   plays |   graded | success   | explosive   |\n|---------:|:-------------|--------:|---------:|:----------|:------------|\n|     2024 | backed up    |      61 |       61 | 63.9%     | 18.0%       |\n|     2024 | red zone     |     113 |      110 | 59.1%     | 7.1%        |\n|     2024 | open field   |     544 |      534 | 58.1%     | 16.0%       |\n|     2025 | backed up    |      63 |       63 | 55.6%     | 9.5%        |\n|     2024 | fringe       |     230 |      223 | 53.4%     | 14.8%       |\n|     2025 | open field   |     550 |      549 | 51.2%     | 8.6%        |\n|     2025 | red zone     |     153 |      153 | 47.7%     | 1.3%        |\n|     2025 | fringe       |     221 |      221 | 44.8%     | 8.1%        |\n\n### Quarter\n\n## What this cannot answer\n\n- **Why.** These are counts and rates. They locate the decline; they do not explain it. Offensive line play, receiver separation and defensive quality are all plausible drivers and none is measured here.\n- **Opponent strength.** No adjustment is made for schedule. A success rate against one defence is not comparable to another without it.\n- **Individual players.** The charting file carries no Baylor player identifiers beyond a quarterback jersey number. Player-level attribution needs PFF's participation data joined in, which is a further build.\n- **Score state.** Neither source carries a running in-game score, so trailing, leading and garbage-time splits are not available. Quarter is the closest proxy in this report and is a weak one.\n\n---\n\n## Reproducing this\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA\n.venv/bin/python run.py           # join + both data gates\n.venv/bin/python build_tables.py  # every table in outputs/tables/\n.venv/bin/python build_report.py  # this document\n```\n\n`run.py` exits non-zero if the join match rate falls below 95% or the charting shortfall exceeds 2%. Source data is the season CSVs in this repo plus `PFF_Data_Project/data/pff.db`, which is opened read-only.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"37b98bb5-41d0-4c14-87f2-2f148486ecc7","timestamp":"2026-09-10T03:14:41.590Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Baylor Offensive Play-Call Analysis\n\n## 2024 and 2025, 1,935 charted plays across 25 games\n\n**Audience:** analytics staff. **Scope:** Baylor offensive snaps, 2024 and 2025 only.\n\n---\n\n## The headline\n\nOffensive success rate fell from **57.4%** in 2024 to **49.5%** in 2025 — a drop of **8.0 points**.\n\nExplosive-play rate fell further in relative terms, from **14.8%** to **7.4%** — roughly half.\n\nThe offensive coordinator did not change between these seasons, and Sawyer Robertson took the large majority of snaps at quarterback in both — 806 in 2024, 958 in 2025. The drop is therefore not explained by a change of play-caller or a new starter. It has to be read as scheme, personnel or execution inside one system.\n\n|   season |   plays |   graded | success   | explosive   |\n|---------:|--------:|---------:|:----------|:------------|\n|     2024 |     948 |      928 | 57.4%     | 14.8%       |\n|     2025 |     987 |      986 | 49.5%     | 7.4%        |\n\n---\n\n## Where it broke\n\nFour findings carry most of the decline.\n\n### 1. First down\n\n- **1st & 10** — 60.1% to 43.7%, down **16.4 points**.\n\nThis is the largest single move in the study, and it lands on the most common situation in football — roughly 390 plays in each season, about 40.3% of every snap charted. An offense that cannot win first down spends the rest of the series behind schedule.\n\n### 2. The run game\n\n- **Run** — 63.1% to 50.7%, down **12.4 points**.\n- **Pass** — 52.8% to 49.3%, down **3.5 points**.\n\nThe pass game moved only slightly. The run game fell off a cliff. In 2024 running was clearly the better option; by 2025 the two were level, and the offense had lost the thing it did best.\n\n|   season | run/pass   |   plays |   graded | success   | explosive   |\n|---------:|:-----------|--------:|---------:|:----------|:------------|\n|     2024 | R          |     447 |      444 | 63.1%     | 11.6%       |\n|     2024 | P          |     486 |      479 | 52.8%     | 18.1%       |\n|     2025 | R          |     382 |      381 | 50.7%     | 5.8%        |\n|     2025 | P          |     598 |      598 | 49.3%     | 8.5%        |\n\n### 3. Early-down running\n\n- **Run, 1st and 2nd down** — 61.7% to 42.7%, down **19.0 points**.\n\nThis is the run-game decline at its sharpest. Early-down running is where an offense sets up everything that follows, and it is the single worst cut in the study.\n\n|   season | run/pass   |   plays |   graded | success   | explosive   |\n|---------:|:-----------|--------:|---------:|:----------|:------------|\n|     2024 | R          |     349 |      347 | 61.7%     | 11.8%       |\n|     2024 | P          |     356 |      350 | 54.6%     | 17.4%       |\n|     2025 | P          |     438 |      438 | 50.5%     | 9.6%        |\n|     2025 | R          |     296 |      295 | 42.7%     | 4.0%        |\n\n### 4. The offense stopped running\n\nRun share fell from **47.9%** of scrimmage plays to **39.0%**.\n\nCause and effect are not separable from this data alone. The offense may have abandoned a run game that stopped working, or the run game may have suffered from being used less and in more predictable spots. Both readings fit these numbers. Deciding between them needs film, not this table.\n\n\n---\n\n## How much to trust this\n\n- **Join quality.** 98.6% of charting rows matched a PFF play, with 15 of 25 games matching perfectly. Unmatched rows still count toward success rate; they simply carry no PFF defensive fields.\n- **Charting completeness.** The charting file holds essentially every scrimmage play PFF recorded. Worst-case shortfall is **1.6%** (2024, 15 plays); 2025 is complete. If those uncharted 2024 plays were disproportionately unsuccessful, the 2024 rate is very slightly overstated, which would make the real decline smaller — not larger.\n- **No matched play moves backward in game time**, and no charted play is paired with a kickoff, punt, field goal or extra point. Both are enforced as tests and as gates; an earlier version of this pipeline had three charted plays matched to field goals.\n- **1 charted scrimmage play(s) have no PFF counterpart.** Reported separately from the shortfall, never netted against it — a missing play and a surplus play are two different problems and must not cancel.\n- **Seasons 2021 to 2023 are excluded.** Those files carry no down, distance, yardage or field position. Success rate cannot be computed for them at all. Nothing in this report covers them.\n- **Small groups are suppressed.** Any cut with fewer than 15 graded plays is dropped rather than printed. A high rate on a handful of reps is noise.\n- **`Efficiency` is Baylor's own hand-charted success tag**, not a derived metric. Values `X` (victory-formation kneels, penalties) and blank are excluded from the denominator.\n- **This report contains no EPA.** Expected points needs a model fitted on a large play-by-play corpus, which this project does not have. Nothing here is a proxy for EPA, and no number should be read as one.\n- **Coverage is charted by PFF on dropbacks only.** Run plays carry defensive personnel (`def_package`) instead. The two are never pooled. Baylor's own 2025 defensive charting is effectively absent — `Front` 0% filled, `Cover Family` 0.4% — so every 2025 defensive split here comes from PFF.\n- **Cross-year play-call comparison uses base concepts only.** 2025 charts modifiers 2024 did not, so `RAZOR.BLADE SPLIT` and `RAZOR/BLADE` are compared as one concept.\n\n---\n\n## A labelling change you need to know about\n\nThe `Play Type` vocabulary changed between seasons. This was derived from the data, not assumed: each run concept was tracked across both years and its dominant label compared.\n\n| concept         | play_type_2024   | play_type_2025   |   reps_2024 |   reps_2025 | changed   | confidence   |\n|:----------------|:-----------------|:-----------------|------------:|------------:|:----------|:-------------|\n| RAZOR BLADE     | ZONE             | ZONE             |         115 |         122 | False     | high         |\n| IRENE ITALY     | ZONE             | ZONE             |         133 |         108 | False     | high         |\n| MARK MOLLY      | ZONE             | ZONE             |          39 |         100 | False     | high         |\n| GARY GAIL       | CTR              | GAP              |          29 |          70 | True      | high         |\n| RABBIT LETTUCE  | WIDE ZONE        | OZ               |          30 |          39 | True      | high         |\n| COREY CLEVELAND | CTR              | GAP              |          66 |          36 | True      | high         |\n\n`CTR` became `GAP`. `WIDE ZONE` became `OZ`. `ZONE` stayed `ZONE`. These are the same plays under new labels, so any table below that groups by `Play Type` will show 2024 and 2025 categories that look different but are not.\n\n---\n\n## Where the offense won and lost\n\n\n### Down and distance\n\nNote that 3rd & long actually improved. The problem is earlier in the series.\n\n|   season | down & dist   |   plays |   graded | success   | explosive   |\n|---------:|:--------------|--------:|---------:|:----------|:------------|\n### Formation family\n\n|   season | formation   |   plays |   graded | success   | explosive   |\n|---------:|:------------|--------:|---------:|:----------|:------------|\n|     2024 | TRIO        |      15 |       15 | 73.3%     | 26.7%       |\n|     2024 | UNBALANCED  |      46 |       45 | 62.2%     | 4.3%        |\n|     2024 | EMPTY       |      28 |       26 | 61.5%     | 25.0%       |\n|     2024 | DBL STK     |      18 |       18 | 61.1%     | 11.1%       |\n|     2024 | ERL/LTE     |     239 |      237 | 59.9%     | 14.6%       |\n|     2025 | GRN/GLD     |      17 |       17 | 58.8%     | 17.6%       |\n|     2024 | RIP/LIZ     |     202 |      198 | 58.1%     | 14.8%       |\n|     2024 | TRIPS       |     105 |      105 | 57.1%     | 14.3%       |\n|     2025 | UNBAL       |      39 |       39 | 56.4%     | 2.6%        |\n|     2024 | DUO         |     196 |      192 | 55.2%     | 18.4%       |\n|     2025 | DUO         |     291 |      291 | 53.6%     | 6.5%        |\n|     2025 | TRIPS       |     157 |      157 | 51.6%     | 5.7%        |\n|     2025 | RIP/LIZ     |     179 |      179 | 49.7%     | 10.1%       |\n|     2025 | ERL/LTE     |     166 |      166 | 45.2%     | 9.6%        |\n|     2025 | TRIO        |      37 |       37 | 40.5%     | 5.4%        |\n\n### Personnel\n\n|   season | personnel   |   plays |   graded | success   | explosive   |\n|---------:|:------------|--------:|---------:|:----------|:------------|\n|     2024 | 12          |      67 |       66 | 59.1%     | 3.0%        |\n|     2025 | 12S         |      24 |       24 | 58.3%     | 4.2%        |\n|     2024 | 11          |     845 |      832 | 57.8%     | 15.9%       |\n|     2025 | 12          |      18 |       18 | 50.0%     | 5.6%        |\n|     2025 | 11          |     931 |      931 | 49.1%     | 7.6%        |\n|     2024 | 10          |      30 |       29 | 41.4%     | 13.3%       |\n\n### Field zone\n\n|   season | field_zone   |   plays |   graded | success   | explosive   |\n|---------:|:-------------|--------:|---------:|:----------|:------------|\n|     2024 | backed up    |      61 |       61 | 63.9%     | 18.0%       |\n|     2024 | red zone     |     113 |      110 | 59.1%     | 7.1%        |\n|     2024 | open field   |     544 |      534 | 58.1%     | 16.0%       |\n|     2025 | backed up    |      63 |       63 | 55.6%     | 9.5%        |\n|     2024 | fringe       |     230 |      223 | 53.4%     | 14.8%       |\n|     2025 | open field   |     550 |      549 | 51.2%     | 8.6%        |\n|     2025 | red zone     |     153 |      153 | 47.7%     | 1.3%        |\n|     2025 | fringe       |     221 |      221 | 44.8%     | 8.1%        |\n\n### Quarter\n\n## What this cannot answer\n\n- **Why.** These are counts and rates. They locate the decline; they do not explain it. Offensive line play, receiver separation and defensive quality are all plausible drivers and none is measured here.\n- **Opponent strength.** No adjustment is made for schedule. A success rate against one defence is not comparable to another without it.\n- **Individual players.** The charting file carries no Baylor player identifiers beyond a quarterback jersey number. Player-level attribution needs PFF's participation data joined in, which is a further build.\n- **Score state.** Neither source carries a running in-game score, so trailing, leading and garbage-time splits are not available. Quarter is the closest proxy in this report and is a weak one.\n\n---\n\n## Reproducing this\n\n```bash\ncd ~/DEV/PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA\n.venv/bin/python run.py           # join + both data gates\n.venv/bin/python build_tables.py  # every table in outputs/tables/\n.venv/bin/python build_report.py  # this document\n```\n\n`run.py` exits non-zero if the join match rate falls below 95% or the charting shortfall exceeds 2%. Source data is the season CSVs in this repo plus `PFF_Data_Project/data/pff.db`, which is opened read-only.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b60cf0b5-9cf7-490b-9cf3-eb1f2825aab0","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA/.worktrees/phase4-alignment","sessionId":"b8143861-beec-4930-9c27-06bc9d94fa8b","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"main"}
