{"parentUuid":"745c34cc-46c2-444d-8828-66eafedfea71","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"3972e979-5e75-4953-aa06-0c7c970352c1","agentId":"a5c3145ddf1f0dffe","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LjJ7Z3kJQN8MrdQMNCCdu3","type":"tool_result","content":"........................................................................ [ 50%]\n.......................................................................  [100%]\n143 passed in 7.09s\n193:- **`Stunt`** — 12.7% filled in 2024 and 11.4% in 2025. 2025 collapsed to a bare Y on almost every charted row, so the label carries no information.\n194:- **`Shifts`** — 2.5% filled in 2024 and 2.2% in 2025. Too rare in the chart; PFF shift is used instead.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"3979ef3c-fc51-4ce7-a8ee-71a92c837dd4","timestamp":"2026-09-10T03:39:08.957Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"........................................................................ [ 50%]\n.......................................................................  [100%]\n143 passed in 7.09s\n193:- **`Stunt`** — 12.7% filled in 2024 and 11.4% in 2025. 2025 collapsed to a bare Y on almost every charted row, so the label carries no information.\n194:- **`Shifts`** — 2.5% filled in 2024 and 2.2% in 2025. Too rare in the chart; PFF shift is used instead.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"745c34cc-46c2-444d-8828-66eafedfea71","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA/.worktrees/phase4-alignment","sessionId":"b8143861-beec-4930-9c27-06bc9d94fa8b","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"3979ef3c-fc51-4ce7-a8ee-71a92c837dd4","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a5c3145ddf1f0dffe","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14762747 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"442b1fb8-3a21-4a24-9a62-cadd56961399","timestamp":"2026-09-10T03:39:08.961Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14762747 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA/.worktrees/phase4-alignment","sessionId":"b8143861-beec-4930-9c27-06bc9d94fa8b","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"442b1fb8-3a21-4a24-9a62-cadd56961399","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a5c3145ddf1f0dffe","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Ceu4cCZhvhwAKVAAamdSJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RbeGiqvHtiJdyjnAm9KZV9","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport pathlib\np = pathlib.Path(\"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-09-playcalls-phase4-alignment-design.md\")\ns = p.read_text()\ns = s.replace(\n\"\"\"Fill rates were measured on the raw season CSVs and on the PFF `team_play` payloads.\nThe full audit ships as `outputs/tables/alignment_column_audit.csv`. Summary and\nverdicts:\"\"\",\n\"\"\"Fill rates were measured on the raw season CSVs and on the PFF `team_play` payloads.\nThe full audit ships as `outputs/tables/alignment_column_audit.csv`. Summary and\nverdicts:\n\n**Note on the two sets of numbers.** The tables below are measured on the raw\nCSVs. `alignment_column_audit.csv` and the report measure the same columns\n*after* `load_season` drops the nine 2024 drill rows, so a few 2024 figures differ\nby a tenth of a point (`Cover Family` 90.1% raw vs 90.0% cleaned, `Front Adjust`\n32.0% vs 32.2%). The cleaned figures are the ones any analysis actually rests on.\nNeither set is typed into prose anywhere: the audit measures on every build, and\n`COLUMN_VERDICTS` holds only the verdict text, with no figure in it.\"\"\")\np.write_text(s)\nprint(\"patched\")\nPY\n.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q 2>&1 | tail -3\ngit add -A && git commit -q -F - <<'EOF' && git log --oneline -1\nStop typing fill rates into prose; measure them at render time\n\nVerification caught the rule this project has already been burned by. The\nreport's exclusion section read its percentages out of the reason strings in\nCOLUMN_VERDICTS - which are hand-written constants - and the tight-end bullet\nhad 54.3% and 40.3% typed straight into the renderer. Nine figures in the\npublished prose had no table behind them, which is exactly how Phase 1 shipped\ntwo wrong numbers with 68 tests passing.\n\nThe reason strings now say only what is wrong. The fill rates beside them are\nmeasured by column_audit on every build and carried through provenance, so a\nre-export moves them instead of silently invalidating them. A test asserts\nthat no verdict reason contains a figure at all, with season labels excepted\nbecause a year names a thing rather than measuring one.\n\nA second test changes a measured input and asserts the prose moves with it in\nboth directions, which is what actually proves the figure is not baked in.\n\nAlso fixes str.capitalize() flattening acronyms in those reasons - it had\nturned \"a bare Y\" into \"a bare y\" and \"PFF shift\" into \"pff shift\" - and notes\nin the spec why the audit's 2024 fill rates differ from the raw-CSV ones by a\ntenth of a point: load_season drops the nine drill rows first, and the cleaned\nfigure is the one every analysis actually rests on.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Cpiq4uQvzv6fct94zkaVCX\nEOF","description":"Note the two fill measurements in the spec and commit"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1299,"cache_read_input_tokens":236137,"output_tokens":1148,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1299},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1148,"cache_read_input_tokens":236137,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1299,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1299,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Ceu4cBMXChD96SXXZNrg5","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","attributionSkill":"superpowers:using-git-worktrees","attributionPlugin":"superpowers","type":"assistant","uuid":"f205e09f-893e-4af7-a9cd-9d068c5fd198","timestamp":"2026-09-10T03:39:26.183Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA/.worktrees/phase4-alignment","sessionId":"b8143861-beec-4930-9c27-06bc9d94fa8b","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"f205e09f-893e-4af7-a9cd-9d068c5fd198","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"3972e979-5e75-4953-aa06-0c7c970352c1","agentId":"a5c3145ddf1f0dffe","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RbeGiqvHtiJdyjnAm9KZV9","type":"tool_result","content":"patched\n........................................................................ [ 50%]\n.......................................................................  [100%]\n143 passed in 7.17s\n41f8706 Stop typing fill rates into prose; measure them at render time","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"14b65c0c-5f5f-4369-acb6-0627fc26128f","timestamp":"2026-09-10T03:39:33.813Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"patched\n........................................................................ [ 50%]\n.......................................................................  [100%]\n143 passed in 7.17s\n41f8706 Stop typing fill rates into prose; measure them at render time","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f205e09f-893e-4af7-a9cd-9d068c5fd198","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA/.worktrees/phase4-alignment","sessionId":"b8143861-beec-4930-9c27-06bc9d94fa8b","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"14b65c0c-5f5f-4369-acb6-0627fc26128f","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a5c3145ddf1f0dffe","attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA/.worktrees/phase4-alignment/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-09-playcalls-phase4-alignment-design.md","snippet":"1\t# Phase 4 — Alignment and Formation vs Play-Call Outcome — Design\n2\t\n3\t**Date:** 2026-09-09\n4\t**Branch:** `phase4-alignment`\n5\t**Status:** design approved by decision-record (no human gate available; see §0)\n6\t**Builds on:** `2026-09-09-playcalls-success-rate-epa-design.md` (Phase 1) and\n7\t`outputs/REPORT.md`.\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t## 0. On approval gates\n12\t\n13\tThe brainstorming skill requires a human to approve the design before\n14\timplementation. No human is available in this session. Every point where approval\n15\twould have been sought is instead written here as a **Decision** with its evidence\n16\tand its reasoning, and implementation continues. Decisions are numbered `D1`…`D14`\n17\tso a reviewer can accept or reject them individually. Assumptions that are *not*\n18\tbacked by evidence in this repository are marked **ASSUMPTION** in bold.\n19\t\n20\t---\n21\t\n22\t## 1. The question\n23\t\n24\tPhase 1 established, for Baylor offence 2024 → 2025 (same coordinator, same starting\n25\tquarterback):\n26\t\n27\t| Cut | 2024 | 2025 |\n28\t|---|---|---|\n29\t| All plays, success rate | 57.4% | 49.5% |\n30\t| Explosive rate | 14.8% | 7.4% |\n31\t| Early-down run success | 61.7% | 42.7% |\n32\t| Unpressured dropback success | 59.9% | 53.8% |\n33\t\n34\tPhase 1 stopped there and said the cause was not decidable from its data.\n35\t\n36\tPhase 4 asks one question with two competing answers:\n37\t\n38\t- **Mix shift** — Baylor lined up in worse-performing looks more often in 2025, or\n39\t  defences lined up against them differently, and the aggregate fell because the\n40\t  *weights* changed.\n41\t- **Efficacy drop** — the same look running the same concept simply produced less,\n42\t  and the aggregate fell because the *rates* changed.\n43\t\n44\tThese are separable arithmetically. §6 does it.\n45\t\n46\t**Phase 4 is not asked to explain *why* efficacy fell.** If the answer is \"the mix\n47\tbarely moved and the same looks got worse\", that is the finding, and it is reported\n48\tas such rather than dressed up.\n49\t\n50\t---\n51\t\n52\t## 2. Scope\n53\t\n54\t**In scope.** Pre-snap alignment and formation, offence and defence, 2024 and 2025\n55\tonly, success rate as the outcome, explosive rate as a secondary outcome.\n56\t\n57\t**Out of scope.** 2021–2023 (no outcome columns at all — CLAUDE.md, and Phase 1 §2).\n58\tEPA (Phase 2 owns it; §7 explains how it drops in). Player-level attribution.\n59\tOpponent-strength adjustment. Anything requiring film.\n60\t\n61\t---\n62\t\n63\t## 3. Open data question resolved: what `offense_package` means\n64\t\n65\tThe task required this be resolved empirically and not guessed.\n66\t\n67\t### 3.1 The documentation does not answer it\n68\t\n69\t`PFF_Data_Project/docs/swagger/premium-feeds.yaml` declares `offense_package` and\n70\t`defense_package` as bare `type: string` with no description\n71\t(lines 1942 and 1946). `PFF_API_Reference_2026-09-06.md` and `pff_api_notes.md`\n72\tmention `off_package` / `def_package` only in a field list. **No data dictionary for\n73\tthis field exists in that repository.** It had to be derived.\n74\t\n75\t### 3.2 Evidence\n76\t\n77\tAcross all 4,517 `team_play` rows in `pff.db` (every team, not just Baylor), parsing\n78\t`offense_package` as four hyphen-separated integers:\n79\t\n80\t| Slot | Range observed | Rows non-zero (of 3,755 parseable) |\n81\t|---|---|---|\n82\t| 1 | 0–5 | 3,735 |\n83\t| 2 | 0–1 | 60 |\n84\t| 3 | 0–2 | 3,703 |\n85\t| 4 | 0–4 | 3,461 |\n86\t\n87\tThe four numbers sum to **5** on 3,628 of 3,755 rows.\n88\t\n89\tOn the 1,907 Baylor plays that join to the Phase 1 table, `offense_players` contains\n90\texactly **11 jersey numbers on every single row**, and the slot sum is 5 on 1,906 of\n91\tthem. Eleven players minus five offensive linemen minus one quarterback leaves five\n92\tskill players. The vector counts those five.\n93\t\n94\tCross-tabulating against the chart's `Personnel` column (standard RB-TE notation)\n95\tgives an unambiguous mapping:\n96\t\n97\t| `offense_package` | Dominant chart `Personnel` | n | Reading |\n98\t|---|---|---|---|\n99\t| `3-0-1-1` | `11` | 1,743 / 1,749 | 3 WR, 1 RB, 1 TE |\n100\t| `2-0-1-2` | `12` family | 106 / 110 | 2 WR, 1 RB, 2 TE |\n101\t| `4-0-1-0` | `10` | 30 / 30 | 4 WR, 1 RB, 0 TE |\n102\t| `1-0-1-3` | `13` | 7 / 7 | 1 WR, 1 RB, 3 TE |\n103\t\n104\t**Decision D1 — the slot order is `WR - FB - RB - TE`, counting the five non-OL,\n105\tnon-QB players on the field.**\n106\t\n107\tSlots 1, 3 and 4 are proven: `13` personnel puts its 3 in slot 4, which rules out\n108\tslot 2 being the tight-end slot; `10` personnel puts 4 in slot 1 and 0 in slot 4,\n109\tfixing WR and TE; the remaining 1 is the back, in slot 3.\n110\t\n111\tSlot 2 is **inferred**, not proven. It never exceeds 1 and is non-zero on 60 of\n112\t3,755 league-wide rows, which is the signature of a fullback / H-back. **ASSUMPTION:\n113\tslot 2 = FB.** It is immaterial to this analysis: it is non-zero on **1 of 2,311**\n114\tBaylor snaps.\n115\t\n116\t### 3.3 The suffixes\n117\t\n118\tValues carry trailing annotations. Every `(*)`-suffixed value sums to **less than 5**,\n119\tand the deficit equals the number of asterisks:\n120\t\n121\t| Value | Slot sum | Suffix | Sum + implied |\n122\t|---|---|---|---|\n123\t| `2-1-1-0 (*)` | 4 | one `*` | 5 |\n124\t| `1-1-1-0 (**)` | 3 | two `*` | 5 |\n125\t| `2-0-1-1 (+Q)` | 4 | `+Q` | 5 |\n126\t| `X-X-X-X` | — | — | unidentified play |\n127\t\n128\t**Decision D2 — `(*)` means that many players PFF could not assign a position;\n129\t`(+Q)` means a second quarterback occupied one of the five skill slots; `X-X-X-X`\n130\tmeans the personnel was not identified.** These are stripped before parsing and the\n131\trow is recorded as `personnel_pff = \"OTHER\"` rather than being silently coerced.\n132\t\n133\t### 3.4 Consequence for the analysis\n134\t\n135\t**Decision D3 — the chart's `Personnel` column is the primary personnel field;\n136\t`offense_package` is a cross-check, not a grouping dimension.**\n137\t\n138\tReasoning: chart `Personnel` is **100% filled in both seasons**; `offense_package` is\n139\t99.4% / 97.8% and is missing precisely on the plays that failed the join. The chart is\n140\talso the source Baylor staff read. The two agree on ~98.7% of joined plays.\n141\t\n142\tThe residue is interesting but is **not a finding**: `4-0-0-1` (n=4) and `3-0-0-2`\n143\t(n=4) are zero-RB packages that the chart calls `11`. The likely reading is that PFF\n144\tcounts by *where the player aligned* and the chart counts by *who was on the field* —\n145\ta back split wide becomes a receiver to PFF. n < 10; recorded, not claimed.\n146\t\n147\tEvidence is emitted as `outputs/tables/alignment_offense_package_crosswalk.csv` so the\n148\tclaim above is reproducible rather than asserted.\n149\t\n150\t---\n151\t\n152\t## 4. Second open data question resolved: `mofo_coverage_played_shown`\n153\t\n154\tThis field is the only defensive-look field with near-complete fill in **both**\n155\tseasons and on **both runs and passes**. That makes it load-bearing, so its two\n156\tcharacters had to be resolved before use.\n157\t\n158\tValues are two characters from `{O, C}` — middle-of-field Open / Closed. Fill is\n159\t97.9% (2024) and 96.9% (2025); on runs specifically it is 99.8% / 97.1%, so unlike\n160\tPFF `coverage_type` it is **not** dropbacks-only (CLAUDE.md trap 3 does not bite here).\n161\t\n162\t### 4.1 Evidence\n163\t\n164\tBaylor's own 2024 `Cover Family` column is 90.1% filled on all plays. Mapping it to\n165\tmiddle-of-field state by convention (`0`, `1`, `3` → closed; `2`, `4`, `6` → open) and\n166\tcross-tabulating each character against it, over the 826 plays where both exist:\n167\t\n168\t| Character | Agreement with charted coverage family |\n169\t|---|---|\n170\t| First (`c1`) | 554 / 826 = **67.1%** |\n171\t| Second (`c2`) | 758 / 826 = **91.8%** |\n172\t\n173\tBaylor's coaches chart the coverage that was **played**, not the one shown. The second\n174\tcharacter is therefore the played coverage and the first is the pre-snap shown look.\n175\t\n176\tA second, independent line of evidence agrees: `OC` (first char open, second closed)\n177\toccurs 419 times across both seasons and `CO` only 156. Defences showing two-high and\n178\trotating down to a closed middle is roughly three times as common as the reverse,\n179\twhich is the well-documented modern pattern. If `c1` were the played coverage the\n180\tasymmetry would run the other way.\n181\t\n\n... [359 lines truncated] ..."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"c0468883-fb4d-4177-836a-d44bd1fd4ee4","timestamp":"2026-09-10T03:39:34.029Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/kristinarash/DEV/PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA/.worktrees/phase4-alignment/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-09-playcalls-phase4-alignment-design.md changed on disk since you last read it. That's usually deliberate, so take it as the current state rather than reverting it; if the change looks wrong, say so rather than undoing it yourself — otherwise no need to call it out. Here are the relevant changes (shown with line numbers):\n1\t# Phase 4 — Alignment and Formation vs Play-Call Outcome — Design\n2\t\n3\t**Date:** 2026-09-09\n4\t**Branch:** `phase4-alignment`\n5\t**Status:** design approved by decision-record (no human gate available; see §0)\n6\t**Builds on:** `2026-09-09-playcalls-success-rate-epa-design.md` (Phase 1) and\n7\t`outputs/REPORT.md`.\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t## 0. On approval gates\n12\t\n13\tThe brainstorming skill requires a human to approve the design before\n14\timplementation. No human is available in this session. Every point where approval\n15\twould have been sought is instead written here as a **Decision** with its evidence\n16\tand its reasoning, and implementation continues. Decisions are numbered `D1`…`D14`\n17\tso a reviewer can accept or reject them individually. Assumptions that are *not*\n18\tbacked by evidence in this repository are marked **ASSUMPTION** in bold.\n19\t\n20\t---\n21\t\n22\t## 1. The question\n23\t\n24\tPhase 1 established, for Baylor offence 2024 → 2025 (same coordinator, same starting\n25\tquarterback):\n26\t\n27\t| Cut | 2024 | 2025 |\n28\t|---|---|---|\n29\t| All plays, success rate | 57.4% | 49.5% |\n30\t| Explosive rate | 14.8% | 7.4% |\n31\t| Early-down run success | 61.7% | 42.7% |\n32\t| Unpressured dropback success | 59.9% | 53.8% |\n33\t\n34\tPhase 1 stopped there and said the cause was not decidable from its data.\n35\t\n36\tPhase 4 asks one question with two competing answers:\n37\t\n38\t- **Mix shift** — Baylor lined up in worse-performing looks more often in 2025, or\n39\t  defences lined up against them differently, and the aggregate fell because the\n40\t  *weights* changed.\n41\t- **Efficacy drop** — the same look running the same concept simply produced less,\n42\t  and the aggregate fell because the *rates* changed.\n43\t\n44\tThese are separable arithmetically. §6 does it.\n45\t\n46\t**Phase 4 is not asked to explain *why* efficacy fell.** If the answer is \"the mix\n47\tbarely moved and the same looks got worse\", that is the finding, and it is reported\n48\tas such rather than dressed up.\n49\t\n50\t---\n51\t\n52\t## 2. Scope\n53\t\n54\t**In scope.** Pre-snap alignment and formation, offence and defence, 2024 and 2025\n55\tonly, success rate as the outcome, explosive rate as a secondary outcome.\n56\t\n57\t**Out of scope.** 2021–2023 (no outcome columns at all — CLAUDE.md, and Phase 1 §2).\n58\tEPA (Phase 2 owns it; §7 explains how it drops in). Player-level attribution.\n59\tOpponent-strength adjustment. Anything requiring film.\n60\t\n61\t---\n62\t\n63\t## 3. Open data question resolved: what `offense_package` means\n64\t\n65\tThe task required this be resolved empirically and not guessed.\n66\t\n67\t### 3.1 The documentation does not answer it\n68\t\n69\t`PFF_Data_Project/docs/swagger/premium-feeds.yaml` declares `offense_package` and\n70\t`defense_package` as bare `type: string` with no description\n71\t(lines 1942 and 1946). `PFF_API_Reference_2026-09-06.md` and `pff_api_notes.md`\n72\tmention `off_package` / `def_package` only in a field list. **No data dictionary for\n73\tthis field exists in that repository.** It had to be derived.\n74\t\n75\t### 3.2 Evidence\n76\t\n77\tAcross all 4,517 `team_play` rows in `pff.db` (every team, not just Baylor), parsing\n78\t`offense_package` as four hyphen-separated integers:\n79\t\n80\t| Slot | Range observed | Rows non-zero (of 3,755 parseable) |\n81\t|---|---|---|\n82\t| 1 | 0–5 | 3,735 |\n83\t| 2 | 0–1 | 60 |\n84\t| 3 | 0–2 | 3,703 |\n85\t| 4 | 0–4 | 3,461 |\n86\t\n87\tThe four numbers sum to **5** on 3,628 of 3,755 rows.\n88\t\n89\tOn the 1,907 Baylor plays that join to the Phase 1 table, `offense_players` contains\n90\texactly **11 jersey numbers on every single row**, and the slot sum is 5 on 1,906 of\n91\tthem. Eleven players minus five offensive linemen minus one quarterback leaves five\n92\tskill players. The vector counts those five.\n93\t\n94\tCross-tabulating against the chart's `Personnel` column (standard RB-TE notation)\n95\tgives an unambiguous mapping:\n96\t\n97\t| `offense_package` | Dominant chart `Personnel` | n | Reading |\n98\t|---|---|---|---|\n99\t| `3-0-1-1` | `11` | 1,743 / 1,749 | 3 WR, 1 RB, 1 TE |\n100\t| `2-0-1-2` | `12` family | 106 / 110 | 2 WR, 1 RB, 2 TE |\n101\t| `4-0-1-0` | `10` | 30 / 30 | 4 WR, 1 RB, 0 TE |\n102\t| `1-0-1-3` | `13` | 7 / 7 | 1 WR, 1 RB, 3 TE |\n103\t\n104\t**Decision D1 — the slot order is `WR - FB - RB - TE`, counting the five non-OL,\n105\tnon-QB players on the field.**\n106\t\n107\tSlots 1, 3 and 4 are proven: `13` personnel puts its 3 in slot 4, which rules out\n108\tslot 2 being the tight-end slot; `10` personnel puts 4 in slot 1 and 0 in slot 4,\n109\tfixing WR and TE; the remaining 1 is the back, in slot 3.\n110\t\n111\tSlot 2 is **inferred**, not proven. It never exceeds 1 and is non-zero on 60 of\n112\t3,755 league-wide rows, which is the signature of a fullback / H-back. **ASSUMPTION:\n113\tslot 2 = FB.** It is immaterial to this analysis: it is non-zero on **1 of 2,311**\n114\tBaylor snaps.\n115\t\n116\t### 3.3 The suffixes\n117\t\n118\tValues carry trailing annotations. Every `(*)`-suffixed value sums to **less than 5**,\n119\tand the deficit equals the number of asterisks:\n120\t\n121\t| Value | Slot sum | Suffix | Sum + implied |\n122\t|---|---|---|---|\n123\t| `2-1-1-0 (*)` | 4 | one `*` | 5 |\n124\t| `1-1-1-0 (**)` | 3 | two `*` | 5 |\n125\t| `2-0-1-1 (+Q)` | 4 | `+Q` | 5 |\n126\t| `X-X-X-X` | — | — | unidentified play |\n127\t\n128\t**Decision D2 — `(*)` means that many players PFF could not assign a position;\n129\t`(+Q)` means a second quarterback occupied one of the five skill slots; `X-X-X-X`\n130\tmeans the personnel was not identified.** These are stripped before parsing and the\n131\trow is recorded as `personnel_pff = \"OTHER\"` rather than being silently coerced.\n132\t\n133\t### 3.4 Consequence for the analysis\n134\t\n135\t**Decision D3 — the chart's `Personnel` column is the primary personnel field;\n136\t`offense_package` is a cross-check, not a grouping dimension.**\n137\t\n138\tReasoning: chart `Personnel` is **100% filled in both seasons**; `offense_package` is\n139\t99.4% / 97.8% and is missing precisely on the plays that failed the join. The chart is\n140\talso the source Baylor staff read. The two agree on ~98.7% of joined plays.\n141\t\n142\tThe residue is interesting but is **not a finding**: `4-0-0-1` (n=4) and `3-0-0-2`\n143\t(n=4) are zero-RB packages that the chart calls `11`. The likely reading is that PFF\n144\tcounts by *where the player aligned* and the chart counts by *who was on the field* —\n145\ta back split wide becomes a receiver to PFF. n < 10; recorded, not claimed.\n146\t\n147\tEvidence is emitted as `outputs/tables/alignment_offense_package_crosswalk.csv` so the\n148\tclaim above is reproducible rather than asserted.\n149\t\n150\t---\n151\t\n152\t## 4. Second open data question resolved: `mofo_coverage_played_shown`\n153\t\n154\tThis field is the only defensive-look field with near-complete fill in **both**\n155\tseasons and on **both runs and passes**. That makes it load-bearing, so its two\n156\tcharacters had to be resolved before use.\n157\t\n158\tValues are two characters from `{O, C}` — middle-of-field Open / Closed. Fill is\n159\t97.9% (2024) and 96.9% (2025); on runs specifically it is 99.8% / 97.1%, so unlike\n160\tPFF `coverage_type` it is **not** dropbacks-only (CLAUDE.md trap 3 does not bite here).\n161\t\n162\t### 4.1 Evidence\n163\t\n164\tBaylor's own 2024 `Cover Family` column is 90.1% filled on all plays. Mapping it to\n165\tmiddle-of-field state by convention (`0`, `1`, `3` → closed; `2`, `4`, `6` → open) and\n166\tcross-tabulating each character against it, over the 826 plays where both exist:\n167\t\n168\t| Character | Agreement with charted coverage family |\n169\t|---|---|\n170\t| First (`c1`) | 554 / 826 = **67.1%** |\n171\t| Second (`c2`) | 758 / 826 = **91.8%** |\n172\t\n173\tBaylor's coaches chart the coverage that was **played**, not the one shown. The second\n174\tcharacter is therefore the played coverage and the first is the pre-snap shown look.\n175\t\n176\tA second, independent line of evidence agrees: `OC` (first char open, second closed)\n177\toccurs 419 times across both seasons and `CO` only 156. Defences showing two-high and\n178\trotating down to a closed middle is roughly three times as common as the reverse,\n179\twhich is the well-documented modern pattern. If `c1` were the played coverage the\n180\tasymmetry would run the other way.\n181\t\n\n... [359 lines truncated] ...\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA/.worktrees/phase4-alignment","sessionId":"b8143861-beec-4930-9c27-06bc9d94fa8b","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"main"}
