{"parentUuid":"48ed5ab7-c291-4f5c-a96d-c8aa762b4afd","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a5c3145ddf1f0dffe","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14502502 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"638fea40-68ca-4087-b536-d29a449de514","timestamp":"2026-09-10T03:44:13.509Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14502502 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA/.worktrees/phase4-alignment","sessionId":"b8143861-beec-4930-9c27-06bc9d94fa8b","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"638fea40-68ca-4087-b536-d29a449de514","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a5c3145ddf1f0dffe","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Ceu4zfZGr8yuPa6pRLqwi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013h4zhfjZJcf3stecVDDFHL","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'PY'\nimport pathlib\np = pathlib.Path(\"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-09-playcalls-phase4-alignment-design.md\")\ns = p.read_text()\ns = s.replace(\n\"\"\"Baylor's coaches chart the coverage that was **played**, not the one shown. The second\ncharacter is therefore the played coverage and the first is the pre-snap shown look.\"\"\",\n\"\"\"**ASSUMPTION: Baylor's coaches chart the coverage that was played, not the one\nshown.** This is the hinge of the whole reading. If they charted the pre-snap shell\ninstead, the same 91.8% agreement would mean character 2 is the *shown* look and the\nfield's own word order was right after all. The assumption is well founded — the\n`Cover Family` values `0`/`1`/`2`/`3`/`4`/`6` are post-snap coverage families, which\nis what a coach grades a play against — but it is an assumption, not a measurement,\nand it is marked as one here and in the report.\n\nTaking it, the second character is the played coverage and the first is the pre-snap\nshown look.\"\"\")\ns = s.replace(\n\"\"\"**Decision D11 — all three terms are reported, never two.** The two-term Oaxaca forms\nbury the interaction inside whichever term is evaluated at year-1 weights, which\nflatters whichever story the analyst already believes. The three terms sum to Δ\nexactly; **a test asserts the identity to 1e-9 on real data and on synthetic data.**\"\"\",\n\"\"\"**Decision D11 — all three terms are reported, never two.** The two-term Oaxaca forms\nbury the interaction inside whichever term is evaluated at year-1 weights, which\nflatters whichever story the analyst already believes. The three terms sum to Δ\nexactly; **a test asserts the identity to 1e-9 on real data and on synthetic data.**\n\n**Decision D11a — the null-result verdict is guarded on `mix + interaction`, not on\n`mix` alone.** The counterfactual the report actually states — hold 2025 usage at its\n2024 level and see what happens — is Σp₀·r₁ − Σp₁·r₁ = Σ(p₁−p₀)·r₁, which expands to\n*mix plus interaction*. A guard on `|mix|` would let a negligible mix with a large\ninteraction through as a null result. On this data both are small (the largest\ncombined weights effect on any dimension is about 1.4 points against an eight-point\nefficacy term), so the verdict is unchanged — but the guard now tests the sentence it\nis guarding, which matters once Phase 2's EPA replaces success rate.\"\"\")\np.write_text(s)\nprint(\"patched\")\nPY\n.venv/bin/python build_alignment_tables.py >/dev/null && .venv/bin/python build_alignment_report.py >/dev/null && sed -n '/^## The verdict/,/^## Decomposition/p' outputs/ALIGNMENT.md","description":"Add spec assumption markers and rebuild the report"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1063,"cache_read_input_tokens":254845,"output_tokens":1062,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1063},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1062,"cache_read_input_tokens":254845,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1063,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1063,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Ceu4zeVmnkouPGWGN7ua6","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","attributionSkill":"superpowers:using-git-worktrees","attributionPlugin":"superpowers","type":"assistant","uuid":"87050ee5-7741-4665-bcc3-a83a30ebcbde","timestamp":"2026-09-10T03:44:26.534Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-1","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":null,"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA/.worktrees/phase4-alignment","sessionId":"b8143861-beec-4930-9c27-06bc9d94fa8b","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"87050ee5-7741-4665-bcc3-a83a30ebcbde","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"3972e979-5e75-4953-aa06-0c7c970352c1","agentId":"a5c3145ddf1f0dffe","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013h4zhfjZJcf3stecVDDFHL","type":"tool_result","content":"patched\n## The verdict\n\nOverall success rate fell from **57.4%** to **49.5%**, a drop of **7.9 points**. Splitting that change into the part caused by Baylor and its opponents lining up differently (**mix**) and the part caused by the same alignments producing less (**efficacy**):\n\n| Term | Points |\n|---|---|\n| Mix — the weights moved | -0.5 |\n| Efficacy — the rates moved | -8.8 |\n| Interaction | 1.3 |\n| **Total change** | **-7.9** |\n\n**Alignment mix does not explain the decline.** Across every dimension tested, the largest mix term anywhere is 1.1 points — on `mofo_shown` — against a typical efficacy term of 8.0 points. Adding the interaction, which is the other half of what changing the weights would do, the largest combined effect of the weights on any dimension is 1.4 points, on `def_package_norm`. Baylor did not line up meaningfully differently in 2025, defences did not line up meaningfully differently against them, and if you had held the 2025 usage of every alignment at its 2024 level the season would have looked almost exactly the same.\n\nThat is a null result on the mix hypothesis, and it is reported as one. The decline is an efficacy drop: the same looks, in the same proportions, produced less.\n\nThe weights are called negligible here when the larger of the mix term and the mix-plus-interaction term is more than 3x smaller than the typical efficacy term. The threshold is stated so it can be disagreed with.\n\n### The sharpest cut Phase 1 found\n\nEarly-down running fell from 61.7% to 42.7%. Decomposed by run concept — the best-powered cut in this phase, with all 5 groups clearing the low-n floor in both seasons — the split is -0.8 points of mix against -18.0 points of efficacy. Baylor called much the same concepts in much the same proportions. Every one of them produced less.\n\n---\n\n## Decomposition, every dimension and subset","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"b4f06d2e-d01c-4048-9668-5cca429ea399","timestamp":"2026-09-10T03:44:28.469Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"patched\n## The verdict\n\nOverall success rate fell from **57.4%** to **49.5%**, a drop of **7.9 points**. Splitting that change into the part caused by Baylor and its opponents lining up differently (**mix**) and the part caused by the same alignments producing less (**efficacy**):\n\n| Term | Points |\n|---|---|\n| Mix — the weights moved | -0.5 |\n| Efficacy — the rates moved | -8.8 |\n| Interaction | 1.3 |\n| **Total change** | **-7.9** |\n\n**Alignment mix does not explain the decline.** Across every dimension tested, the largest mix term anywhere is 1.1 points — on `mofo_shown` — against a typical efficacy term of 8.0 points. Adding the interaction, which is the other half of what changing the weights would do, the largest combined effect of the weights on any dimension is 1.4 points, on `def_package_norm`. Baylor did not line up meaningfully differently in 2025, defences did not line up meaningfully differently against them, and if you had held the 2025 usage of every alignment at its 2024 level the season would have looked almost exactly the same.\n\nThat is a null result on the mix hypothesis, and it is reported as one. The decline is an efficacy drop: the same looks, in the same proportions, produced less.\n\nThe weights are called negligible here when the larger of the mix term and the mix-plus-interaction term is more than 3x smaller than the typical efficacy term. The threshold is stated so it can be disagreed with.\n\n### The sharpest cut Phase 1 found\n\nEarly-down running fell from 61.7% to 42.7%. Decomposed by run concept — the best-powered cut in this phase, with all 5 groups clearing the low-n floor in both seasons — the split is -0.8 points of mix against -18.0 points of efficacy. Baylor called much the same concepts in much the same proportions. Every one of them produced less.\n\n---\n\n## Decomposition, every dimension and subset","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"87050ee5-7741-4665-bcc3-a83a30ebcbde","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/DEV/PlayCalls_SuccessRate_EPA/.worktrees/phase4-alignment","sessionId":"b8143861-beec-4930-9c27-06bc9d94fa8b","version":"2.1.267","gitBranch":"main"}
